1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,552
Take twelve parts off one machine. Same tool,

2
00:00:04,652 --> 00:00:08,404
same operator, same gauge, one after another.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,635
Every reading is different, and nothing is broken. Watch the

4
00:00:13,735 --> 00:00:17,108
span open up as the parts arrive — twelve of

5
00:00:17,208 --> 00:00:21,369
them cover 47 microns, and the closest pair differs by

6
00:00:21,469 --> 00:00:25,316
1 micron, which is exactly where this gauge stops.

7
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:28,348
Below that it has nothing to say.

8
00:00:28,433 --> 00:00:31,998
Spread is not a defect. It is what every real

9
00:00:32,098 --> 00:00:35,581
process does, and the job is to describe it.

10
00:00:37,766 --> 00:00:40,883
Two hundred and forty measurements off

11
00:00:40,983 --> 00:00:43,930
one stable process, piled into bins.

12
00:00:44,516 --> 00:00:48,208
The shape looks like a fact about the process. It is

13
00:00:48,308 --> 00:00:52,219
not. Bin width is a setting on the instrument, and here

14
00:00:52,319 --> 00:00:55,792
is the same data again with the knob turned down.

15
00:00:55,883 --> 00:01:00,871
Four bins says the process is a lump. Turn it the other way.

16
00:01:00,966 --> 00:01:04,718
Fifty two says it is a comb of spikes. Neither is

17
00:01:04,818 --> 00:01:09,434
the process. Somewhere in between is a picture you can read.

18
00:01:11,733 --> 00:01:15,043
Here are the measurements in the order they were

19
00:01:15,143 --> 00:01:18,737
made, and underneath, the same measurements in bins.

20
00:01:18,833 --> 00:01:22,253
Three readouts: the mean, the spread, and the

21
00:01:22,353 --> 00:01:25,069
longest run on one side of the mean.

22
00:01:25,166 --> 00:01:28,833
Now keep every single number and change nothing but

23
00:01:28,933 --> 00:01:31,861
the order they arrived in. This is what a

24
00:01:31,961 --> 00:01:35,554
slow drift looks like written down as it happened.

25
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:39,604
The mean has not moved. The spread has not moved. The

26
00:01:39,704 --> 00:01:44,424
histogram cannot move, because it is the same numbers. Only the

27
00:01:44,524 --> 00:01:49,014
run readout changes, from seven to a hundred and twenty one.

28
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:55,089
Same histogram, and one of those two processes needs an engineer today. That

29
00:01:55,189 --> 00:01:59,656
is why every chart after this one is drawn in time order.

30
00:02:02,050 --> 00:02:06,027
Two runs of the same process, from the same random

31
00:02:06,127 --> 00:02:10,350
draws. The only difference is what the operator does.

32
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:15,871
The operator on the bottom chart corrects after every part, by exactly

33
00:02:15,971 --> 00:02:19,888
what the last part was out by. It is the most reasonable

34
00:02:19,988 --> 00:02:23,834
thing in the world, and watch the two spreads separate.

35
00:02:23,933 --> 00:02:28,513
Every correction adds the last part's noise to this one's. The

36
00:02:28,613 --> 00:02:33,042
variance is exactly doubled, so the spread is root two times

37
00:02:33,142 --> 00:02:37,345
wider — and the operator has been working hard all shift.

38
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:40,806
Noise is not a signal, and answering it is how

39
00:02:40,906 --> 00:02:45,033
you add variation rather than remove it. Telling the two

40
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:48,733
apart is what the rest of this curriculum is for.

